<h1>Adam Adamandy Kochański</h1>
<p>Biography here is a secondary layer, not the spine. The site tells the story of a problem he worked on, not the order of his moves. Below is only what is needed to place the instruments in time — with the points where the scholarship disagrees marked openly.</p>
<h2>Timeline</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>1631</strong> — born in the Dobrzyń region. <em>One museum text gives 1637; 1631 is the prevailing version.</em></li>
<li><strong>1652</strong> — Jesuit novitiate in Vilnius, then studies at the Vilnius Academy.</li>
<li><strong>1660s–1670s</strong> — teaching at European colleges: Florence, Prague, Olomouc, Würzburg, Mainz.</li>
<li><strong>1659</strong> — proposal to replace the pendulum with a regulating spring.</li>
<li><strong>1664</strong> — <em>Mirabilis chronometrica</em>, book IX of Caspar Schott's {% zrodlo "technica-curiosa", "<em>Technica curiosa</em>" %}. <em>Some sources give the title as</em> Mirabilia chronometrica.</li>
<li><strong>1667</strong> — a watch with a magnetic balance, presented to Ferdinand de' Medici.</li>
<li><strong>c. 1672</strong> — a spring regulating the balance wheel in pocket watches.</li>
<li><strong>1678, 1679 or 1680</strong> — arrival at the court of John III Sobieski. <em>Three different dates appear in the literature.</em></li>
</ul>
{% pytanie "A5", jezyk %}
<ul>
<li><strong>c. 1684</strong> — the sundial on the garden front at Wilanów, in correspondence with Hevelius.</li>
<li><strong>1685</strong> — <em>Observationes cyclometricae</em>, {% zrodlo "acta-1685", "<em>Acta Eruditorum</em>" %}.</li>
<li><strong>1691–1700</strong> — correspondence with Leibniz resumed after a gap of some twenty years.</li>
<li><strong>1700</strong> — died on 17 May at Teplice in Bohemia.</li>
</ul>
<h2>What this timeline leaves out</h2>
<p>It does not say that Kochański devoted much of his last years to the search for perpetual motion. It does not say that the magnetic-balance watch was a dead end and that the marine clock was never built. The failures belong to this biography and are listed here deliberately — a site without them would be boasting, not an account of work.</p>
